design/complexity  warn  internal/jit/compile.go:350:1
  owner:       jit::compile#function
  message:     function coordinates 14 collaborators in its package
  requirement: A function SHOULD NOT exceed the limit of its role in lines, statements, cyclomatic complexity, cognitive complexity or nesting depth, and SHOULD NOT be a dependency hub or a high fan-out coordinator. Cyclomatic complexity is one path plus each `if`, `else if`, loop, error handler clause, non-default switch arm and boolean operator (`&&`, `||`). The split is by responsibility: the remedy is smaller functions, never moving the same body elsewhere. One function MUST stay at one abstraction level: intent and mechanics are not mixed in one body, so that it reads as a sequence of steps one level below its name. Mechanical checks SHOULD be preferred for directly expressible rules; heuristic signals SHOULD remain advisory and use deliberately high thresholds.
  context:     Size and branching are signals of a function doing too much, not proof of it: the limits are deliberately high, one finding is reported per function (the kind that matched first, in the order below; all matched kinds are in the evidence), and the remedy is always smaller functions with one reason to change, never the same body moved elsewhere (Martin, Clean Code ch. 3: small functions that do one thing at one level of abstraction). Metrics MUST NOT define universal declaration order, justify automatic reordering or prove single responsibility. Kinds, in report order. `lines`: a function's lines (Go `funlen`; the default 60 for Go, 100 for Rust). `statements`: its statements (`funlen` statements, 40; Rust has no default limit). `cyclomatic`: gocyclo's `min-complexity` (30) for Go and Rust, where clippy's cyclomatic lint was removed; ESLint `complexity` (20), Sonar S1541 (10) and cyclop (10) are lower, which fits a review signal less well than a deliberately high limit. `cognitive`: SonarSource cognitive complexity (30 for Go, 25 for Rust). `nesting`: the deepest block nesting (4). `hub`: a dependency hub (many callers and many callees in a long function) or a high fan-out coordinator. Rust: `if`, `else if`, `if let`, `let ... else`, `while`, `for` and `loop` count as in other languages; a `match` counts its arms other than a wildcard or binding arm; `?` adds nothing; closures and `async` blocks nest what they contain. A function that is one `match` whose arms are single values (in return position) or `return`s is a table in code form, and a body whose only top-level branching is one switch of any arms, nested at most two levels, is a flat dispatch: its many paths do not make it hard to follow. `ignoreDispatch` (on by default) leaves both out of `cyclomatic`, and a table in code form out of `cognitive` and `hub`, because counting them reports tables and visitors that read linearly. A function that is not over a limit but has reached 60% of one is put to a model for the abstraction level (`review`); a finding on a function is never asked again.
  evidence:    callees=["a","b c"] fan_in=2 fan_out=14
  expected:    valid go example `lines-within-the-limit` (sample.go), canonical
                 package sample

                 func total(a, b int) int {
                     sum := a

                     sum += b

                     return sum
                 }
  fingerprint: 95c1bd45cd45
  resolve:     lighthouse review resolve 95c1bd45cd45 --judgment <pass|fail|notApplicable> [--suppress <justification>] [--reason <text>] --attributed-to agent:<id>

design/errors  info (authored warn)  src/lib.rs:7:1
  owner:       m::helper#function
  message:     private helper has one caller
  requirement: Dependency identity MUST be preserved when callers depend on it; context added to an error MUST keep the cause reachable: an error MUST be compared with `errors.Is`, asserted with `errors.As` and wrapped with `%w`. Semantic errors MUST use stable, named error values or types, and semantic categories MUST NOT be created from ad hoc formatted messages alone. Error categories MUST be translated only at their owning boundary. Errors MUST NOT expose private or sensitive process state. Panic MUST be limited to impossible programmer errors, `Must*`-style APIs, or documented hot-path invariants with one recovery boundary; normal runtime failures MUST return errors.
  context:     Adding context must not hide the cause callers depend on, and a panic bypasses the caller's control flow (Martin, Clean Code ch. 7 and G26 Be Precise: use exceptions, not return codes, but keep what they carry). Error text and values are part of the public contract: callers classify failures by identity, not by message text. Kinds, one finding per event. `compare` (Go): an error compared with `==`, `!=` or a `switch`, which fails on a wrapped error (`errorlint` comparison, staticcheck): use `errors.Is`. `nil` and `io.EOF`-style sentinels returned unwrapped by contract are fine, and so is the body of an `Is` method. `assert` (Go): a type assertion or type switch on an error: use `errors.As`. `wrap` (Go): `fmt.Errorf` formatting an error without `%w`. Rust keeps identity through `?` and `From`, which the compiler checks, so these kinds leave it to review. Off by default and on in the strict preset, as clippy's restriction lints and forbidigo are opt-in for the same reason: `panic` and `unwrap` report every place production code can panic, one finding each (forbidigo `^panic$`; clippy `panic`, `unwrap_used`, `expect_used`, `todo`, `unimplemented`, `unreachable`). Go reports a call of `panic`; Rust reports the `panic!`, `unreachable!`, `todo!` and `unimplemented!` macros (`panic`) and any `.unwrap()`, `.expect(..)`, `.unwrap_err()` or `.expect_err(..)` call (`unwrap`; syntactically, on any receiver, as clippy does). The findings are candidates, not violations: impossible programmer errors, `Must*`-style APIs and documented hot-path invariants are allowed by the requirement and stay with review. Test code, entrypoints (`main`, `init`) and functions whose name starts with one of `allowPrefixes` are not reported. In Rust, a library that is not published and that only tests link in (no other package lists it in `[dependencies]`, and it has no binary) is test code. Categories, translation and state are judged: they need the meaning of an error, which no event carries, so the requirement and the examples are the question put to a model.
  expected:    valid rust example `rust-is-judged-by-review` (src/lib.rs), canonical
                 #[derive(PartialEq)]
                 pub enum Failure {
                     Missing,
                 }

                 pub fn is_missing(err: &Failure) -> bool {
                     *err == Failure::Missing
                 }
  fingerprint: 46aa7792468f
  resolve:     lighthouse review resolve 46aa7792468f --judgment <pass|fail|notApplicable> [--suppress <justification>] [--reason <text>] --attributed-to agent:<id>

